## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 0 High · 2 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions**. Five of eight tests assert `output == input` on operations where the input is specifically chosen so no transformation occurs, or the method is an identity by design. These tests inflate coverage while proving nothing.

---

### Critical Findings

#### 1. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Tautological assertion

`GetDisplayName()` simply returns `Name` (line 10 of production code). The test asserts `user.Name == display`, i.e. it asserts a field equals itself. This can **never fail**.

```csharp
// BEFORE — tautological
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);   // Name == Name, always true

// FIX — assert the expected formatted output against a literal
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName should actually format (e.g. "Alice (30)"),
// fix the production code AND assert the formatted string.
```

#### 2. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Self-referential (no-op input)

Input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `ToUpper()` returns `"HELLO"` unchanged, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true. The test never verifies that `ToUpper` actually transforms anything.

```csharp
// BEFORE — already uppercase, ToUpper is a no-op
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// FIX — use lowercase input, assert against a literal
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

#### 3. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential (no-op input)

`Normalize` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. The input `"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercase, so the output equals the input trivially. The test never proves normalization works.

```csharp
// BEFORE — input is already normalized
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// FIX — use an input that requires transformation
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  Alice  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

#### 4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 63–69) — Self-referential (pass-through assertion)

`ValidateEmail` returns the input unchanged when valid. Asserting `input == result` only proves the method is a pass-through — it never tests that invalid input is rejected.

```csharp
// BEFORE — only tests the happy pass-through
var input = "hello@world.com";
var result = Validator.ValidateEmail(input);
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// FIX — assert valid input with a literal, AND test the rejection path
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_InvalidInput_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

#### 5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45–53) — Broken / misleading round-trip

`Mapper.ToDto` **drops** `Id` and `Email` (sets `Age = 0`). `Mapper.ToEntity` **drops** `Id` (sets `Id = 0`) and `Email` (sets `Email = ""`). The test asserts `entity.Id == backToEntity.Id` — that's `1 == 0`, which should **fail**. Either this test is currently failing, or it was never run. The round-trip concept is fundamentally wrong here because the mapping is lossy.

```csharp
// FIX — test each mapping direction independently
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
}
```

---

### Medium Findings

| # | Test | Issue | Fix |
|---|------|-------|-----|
| 6 | `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 27) | Never asserts **reference inequality** — doesn't prove Clone created a new object vs. returning `this`. | Add `Assert.AreNotSame(original, clone);` |
| 7 | All tests | **Zero negative/edge-case coverage** — no null inputs, empty strings, boundary values, or exception paths tested across the entire suite. | Add edge-case tests for each unit (null, empty, whitespace, overflow). |

---

### Positive Observations

- **`Serialize_ThenDeserialize_ProducesOriginal`** (line 9) and **`ToString_ParseBack_Identity`** (line 18) are legitimate round-trip tests where identity *is* the correct property to verify. These are well-structured.
- The suite uses clear AAA separation in every test.
